WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.177
A document store groups related nested
information into an aggregate, such as a profile

00:00:05.177 --> 00:00:10.354
containing positions and education entries. That
representation can match the object an

00:00:10.354 --> 00:00:15.471
application reads or updates, but a document is
not automatically a good search index.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.841
Search asks a different question: which
documents contain a term or satisfy several text

00:00:20.841 --> 00:00:26.061
conditions? An inverted index maps terms to
document identifiers. If bronze appears in

00:00:26.061 --> 00:00:31.524
documents four, nine and twelve, while river
appears in nine and fifteen, intersecting the

00:00:31.524 --> 00:00:34.134
lists finds document nine for an AND query.

00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:39.574
Ranking, tokenization and language handling add
more rules, but the posting-list example

00:00:39.574 --> 00:00:43.777
exposes the core access path without scanning
every document's text.

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:49.105
Time-series data suggests another arrangement.
Store readings under a partition key composed

00:00:49.105 --> 00:00:54.375
of sensor identifier and day, then order records
by observation time inside that partition.

00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:59.588
A request for one sensor's morning readings can
find the partition and scan a bounded time

00:00:59.588 --> 00:01:00.109
interval.

00:01:00.277 --> 00:01:04.971
Hashing the partition key distributes
partitions, while the within-partition ordering

00:01:04.971 --> 00:01:09.830
supports the range query. These are two
different roles, so a hash-based design need not

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:14.855
make every range query impossible. The diagram
should show the device-and-day key selecting

00:01:14.855 --> 00:01:17.340
a partition before the time range is applied.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.546
A query across all devices may need another
access path because it does not identify the

00:01:22.546 --> 00:01:24.024
same bounded destination.

00:01:24.024 --> 00:01:29.229
An application cache stores reusable values that
help build responses. For example, every

00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:34.375
page may include the same leaderboard even
though the rest of each page is personalized.

00:01:34.375 --> 00:01:39.696
Caching that leaderboard avoids repeating its
calculation without requiring the entire HTTP

00:01:39.696 --> 00:01:40.983
response to be shared.

00:01:41.024 --> 00:01:46.239
A reverse-proxy cache operates at a different
layer: it reuses complete eligible HTTP

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:51.515
responses. Both can reduce load, but they have
different keys, owners and invalidation

00:01:51.515 --> 00:01:56.975
rules. Draw the application between the browser
and its value cache, then draw a response

00:01:56.975 --> 00:01:58.754
cache before the application.

00:01:58.754 --> 00:02:03.471
The placement makes clear which work is avoided
by a hit at each layer and which work still

00:02:03.471 --> 00:02:03.886
happens.

00:02:03.886 --> 00:02:09.084
In-memory stores can expose operations on
structures such as sets, counters and sorted
sets

00:02:09.084 --> 00:02:14.112
rather than treating every value as an opaque
string. Updating one leaderboard score can

00:02:14.112 --> 00:02:18.796
then avoid reading, modifying and rewriting the
whole ranking in application code.

00:02:18.886 --> 00:02:24.381
Fast access does not settle durability, however.
Ask whether the ranking is authoritative or

00:02:24.381 --> 00:02:29.398
rebuildable from durable events, what happens
after restart, and whether eviction is

00:02:29.398 --> 00:02:34.774
allowed. Memory is limited, so a cache may
discard values according to an eviction policy.

00:02:34.886 --> 00:02:40.022
Expiry determines how long a cached copy remains
eligible for reuse; it does not guarantee

00:02:40.022 --> 00:02:43.845
that the underlying fact has stayed unchanged
during that interval.

00:02:43.886 --> 00:02:49.043
Files, objects and blocks expose different
interfaces. A network filesystem provides

00:02:49.043 --> 00:02:54.569
familiar paths and file operations, which can be
valuable when an existing program expects

00:02:54.569 --> 00:03:00.094
to open and modify files. An object store
provides named objects through a service API and

00:03:00.094 --> 00:03:04.085
is often convenient for images, recordings and
other large media.

00:03:04.085 --> 00:03:09.203
A block volume exposes lower-level storage on
which a filesystem can be built; it is not

00:03:09.203 --> 00:03:14.379
itself a shared file service. These distinctions
affect application behavior. Replacing a

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:19.672
mounted directory with an object bucket is not
simply a configuration change if the program

00:03:19.672 --> 00:03:24.906
relies on operations or coordination semantics
that the object interface does not provide.

00:03:25.085 --> 00:03:30.287
Consider a recording archive. Store the audio
object under a stable identifier and keep

00:03:30.287 --> 00:03:35.728
searchable metadata in a database. A browser
fetches media through a delivery URL while the

00:03:35.728 --> 00:03:41.169
application queries metadata by date, topic or
owner. This avoids forcing large media bytes

00:03:41.169 --> 00:03:44.158
through the same query path as structured
records.

00:03:44.158 --> 00:03:49.484
But the two stores create a workflow: an upload
can succeed while the metadata transaction

00:03:49.484 --> 00:03:54.749
fails, leaving an abandoned object. Publishing
metadata before the upload is complete can

00:03:54.749 --> 00:04:00.193
expose a broken link. The diagram should include
the publish step after durable upload, plus

00:04:00.193 --> 00:04:01.613
a cleanup or retry path.

00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:06.606
Choosing specialized storage does not remove the
need to coordinate its lifecycle with the

00:04:06.606 --> 00:04:07.926
rest of the application.
